this morning our pushing the limit  series meets a canadian woman who is the  first person ever to complete the  trans-canada trail at more than 16 000  miles it is the longest trail in the  whole wide world spanning hundreds of  walking paths roads and waterways deanne  whalen made the journey over six years  by foot by bicycle and by boat and our  jamie wax caught up with her to talk  about what she's learned  they say to get to know someone  you must walk a mile in their shoes  but to really know deanne whalen and  what she's seen over the past six years  that track would take more than sixteen  thousand miles you journeyed  to three oceans from the atlantic ocean  to the pacific ocean to the arctic ocean  yeah actually it went atlantic arctic  and then pacific in 2015 the 56 year old  filmmaker set out for her hardest  expedition yet traveling the entire  trans-canada trail alone so long in fact  if you were to crisscross the united  states five times you still wouldn't  cover the distance of the trans-canada  trail  the solo pilgrimage is the subject of  weyland's next documentary there's  something very fragile about being alone  out on lake superior or deep in the  woods after a while there's something  that wakes up inside you and you  suddenly feel more connected to life  than you've ever been do you come out of  the low depths of loneliness and  isolation into that moment i never  really was lonely on this journey i felt  a strong sense of connection to the  world that was around me the web of life  i went on this journey not as to prove  some sort of athletic accomplishment in  fact by day 10 i realized jamie that i  hadn't even gone as far as i thought i  could go in one day really right so i  burned my schedule  and i joke with people that that's the  day i dropped the rabbit suit for the  turtle shell  and you know surrendered to how long the  journey was going to take me  no schedule to follow allowed waylon to  travel on her own terms taking breaks  when she needed a rest  when the weather was too dangerous  day 50.  through her lens she captured the rugged  pathways and the tools she needed to  navigate the land  the water and at times the treacherous  conditions on average she spent about  eight months on the trail each year  nice to meet you nice to be on your land  along the way weyland crossed paths with  strangers so she said never failed to  offer her a helping hand  and spent time in indigenous communities  whose ancestral land she was traveling  on that's where i actually found my hope  jamie  in learning a different way of different  relationship to the earth instead of  living on it living with it in a lot of  indigenous communities they think about  seven generations  every time you're making a decision you  think about seven generations into the  future how radical that would be if we  brought that into the corporate  boardroom or into government we're  telling our kids we care  on august 1st whalen's journey finally  came to an end her family and the new  friend she met traveling walked with her  hand in hand as she became the first  person and woman to complete the entire  trail what did it feel like on mile zero  i was definitely crying  and it wasn't because my journey was  over  it was because of all the kindness of  people when you watch the news sometimes  you're quite certain that the whole  world's full of sociopaths and it's not  i put myself in a very vulnerable  position being out there alone for as  long as i was and i met a lot of people  and not once did i not meet kindness on  my journey weyland's next journey  sharing the wisdom she learned on her  adventures in a new book and documentary  both titled 500 days in the wild  nature is a place that we could all turn  to through our isolation to fine healing  i definitely found my hope in human  kindness  all across this land and in what  connects us we're you know  for all of our differences we're all  connected by this land and by the water  we are one for cbs mornings i'm jamie  wax in new york
 

